Banks intermediate funding and create deposits through lending, constrained by capital, liquidity, demand, and policy.|A new loan can create a matching borrower deposit in the bank's books.|Banks can lend without any practical constraint?|No|Yes|Only digitally
Bank assets include loans and investments; liabilities include deposits and funding; income includes interest and fees.|Loan interest received less funding interest contributes net interest income.|Deposits are ordinarily the bank's…?|Liabilities|Operating inventory|Shareholder profit
Deposits, loans, and wholesale funding differ in cost, stability, maturity, and credit exposure.|Short-term wholesale funds finance long-term loans.|This can create…?|Funding maturity risk|Guaranteed liquidity|No interest exposure
Net interest income is interest revenue less interest expense; NIM scales it by a defined average earning-asset base.|NII ₹4 over average earning assets ₹100 gives NIM 4%.|NIM is…?|4%|25%|104%
Fees diversify bank revenue; efficiency ratios compare expenses with defined income.|Costs ₹40 over operating income ₹100 give cost/income 40%.|Lower ratio always proves healthier risk-taking?|No|Yes|Only for large banks
ALM manages repricing, maturity, currency, and liquidity mismatches between assets and liabilities.|Fixed-rate loans funded by resetting deposits face margin pressure as funding rates rise.|Primary exposure is…?|Repricing mismatch|Only share dilution|Only credit scoring
Capital adequacy relates qualifying capital to risk-weighted assets; risk weights differ across exposures.|Capital ₹12 over RWA ₹100 gives a 12% ratio before rule-specific details.|Capital ratio here is…?|12%|8.33%|112%
Bank liquidity manages withdrawals and funding shocks; solvency does not guarantee cash availability.|A solvent bank faces rapid deposit outflows.|It can still suffer…?|Liquidity distress|Only inventory losses|No funding problem
Underwriting assesses repayment capacity, purpose, terms, security, and borrower risks before approval.|A borrower has collateral but weak sustainable cash flow.|Collateral replaces repayment analysis entirely?|No|Yes|Only for mortgages
Retail, SME, and corporate lending differ in borrower information, diversification, documentation, and structures.|An SME depends on one owner and customer.|Assessment should include…?|Concentration and business continuity|Only collateral price|Only company age
Ratings and scores estimate credit risk using specified methods, horizons, and data.|A strong historical score deteriorates after a business shock.|Scores guarantee repayment?|No|Yes|Only from major agencies
Expected credit loss economics link default probability, loss severity, and exposure.|PD 2% × LGD 40% × EAD ₹100 gives simplified expected loss ₹0.8.|Expected loss is…?|₹0.8|₹2|₹40
Collateral and guarantees affect recovery and enforceability, subject to valuation and legal terms.|A guarantee's value depends on guarantor capacity and enforceable obligations.|A guarantee automatically removes credit risk?|No|Yes|Only if signed
Covenants constrain risk; debt-service capacity compares available cash with scheduled payments.|Cash available ₹30 over debt service ₹20 gives DSCR 1.5.|DSCR is…?|1.5|0.67|10
Non-performing classification and provisions follow framework-specific definitions; analyse deterioration and coverage.|A borrower misses payments and its expected recovery weakens.|A provision is cash set aside in a separate account automatically?|No|Yes|Only for banks
Expected-credit-loss accounting uses forward-looking credit shortfalls under the applicable standard, not just incurred defaults.|A performing loan can carry an allowance before default.|Allowance must always wait for actual default?|No|Yes|Only for corporate loans
Restructuring changes debt terms; recovery considers viability, collateral, priority, and execution.|Extending maturity eases payments but does not cure a weak business.|A modified loan is automatically risk-free?|No|Yes|Only if interest is reduced
NBFC, microfinance, and housing-finance models differ in funding, customer risk, and applicable regulation.|A lender relies on wholesale borrowing while financing long-term housing.|Funding structure should be…?|Analysed explicitly|Assumed identical to a deposit bank|Ignored if collateral exists
Loan sales and securitisation transfer claims subject to retained risks, servicing, guarantees, and accounting rules.|An originator retains first-loss exposure after selling loans.|All credit risk necessarily disappears?|No|Yes|Only after cash receipt
